Simpler: use `haystack.match_indices(needle)` and check boundaries around each.

```rust
fn is_ident_byte(b: u8) -> bool { b.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || b == b'_' }

fn identifier_occurs(haystack: &str, needle: &str) -> bool {
    if needle.is_empty() { return false; }
    for (idx, _) in haystack.match_indices(needle) {
        let before_ok = idx == 0 || !is_ident_byte(haystack.as_bytes()[idx - 1]);
        let end = idx + needle.len();
        let after_ok = end == haystack.len() || !is_ident_byte(haystack.as_bytes()[end]);
        if before_ok && after_ok { return true; }
    }
    false
}
```
`match_indices` returns byte indices on char boundaries; checking bytes at idx-1 and end: since needle is ASCII, boundaries are ASCII-safe checks. If before byte is a continuation byte of a multibyte char, is_ascii_alphanumeric is false for >= 0x80, so before_ok = true. That's fine (a multi-byte char before an identifier is not an ident char). Good.

